(GALE ENCYCLOPEDIA OF MEDICINE) '
(MUSCULOSKELETAL DISORDERS, KNEE PAIN AND JOINT STRAIN) '
() '
(Orthopedic Medicine, Patellofemoral Mechanics & Arthritis) '
() '
(Definition: Knee pain is a frequent musculoskeletal complaint arising from) '
(mechanical stress, traumatic injury, inflammatory processes, or degenerate) '
(cartilage in the knee joint. The knee is a complex weight-bearing hinge joint) '
(composed of the femur, tibia, patella, stabilizing ligaments \(ACL, PCL, MCL, LCL\),) '
(fibrocartilage shock absorbers \(menisci\), and protective articular cartilage.) '
() '
(Underlying Etiology and Biomechanical Reasons: Knee discomfort develops from) '
(multiple clinical factors: 1\) Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome \(runner's knee\), caused) '
(by abnormal tracking of the kneecap over the femoral groove due to quadriceps and) '
(vastus medialis imbalances; 2\) Meniscus Tears or Ligamentous Sprains resulting) '
(from sudden twisting, impact, or repetitive deceleration; 3\) Osteoarthritis,) '
(involving progressive wear and loss of articular cartilage cushioning, triggering) '
(joint friction and bone-on-bone stiffness; 4\) Patellar Tendinitis or Bursitis) '
(\(inflammation of fluid-filled sacs surrounding the joint\); 5\) Excess Mechanical) '
(Load, where every pound of excess body weight multiplies compressive joint force) '
(by four to six times during weight-bearing movement.) '
() '
(Immediate Relief Action Plan \(R.I.C.E. Protocol\): For acute knee or joint pain,) '
(immediate conservative management follows the evidence-based R.I.C.E. clinical) '
(protocol: 1\) Rest: Cease strenuous weight-bearing activities immediately to) '
(prevent tissue micro-trauma; 2\) Ice: Apply cold packs wrapped in a thin towel for) '
(15 to 20 minutes every 2 to 3 hours to constrict local blood vessels and reduce) '
(inflammatory effusion; 3\) Compression: Apply an elastic tubular bandage or knee) '
(sleeve with gentle, even compression to limit joint swelling \(avoid wrapping so) '
(tightly as to compromise arterial or venous circulation\); 4\) Elevation: Prop the) '
(leg up on pillows above the level of the heart while resting to promote passive) '
(lymphatic and venous fluid drainage.) '
() '
(Long-Term Cure and Rehabilitation Plan: Lasting recovery and joint preservation) '
(require structured non-pharmacological care: 1\) Targeted Physical Therapy focusing) '
(on quadriceps strengthening \(especially straight leg raises and isometric quad) '
(sets\), hamstring flexibility, and hip abductor stabilization; 2\) Low-Impact) '
(Cardiovascular Conditioning such as swimming or stationary cycling with the saddle) '
(adjusted to avoid hyperflexion; 3\) Weight Optimization to diminish cumulative) '
(compressive cartilage loading; 4\) Proper Supportive Footwear and ergonomic) '
(orthotics if overpronation is present; 5\) Clinical Evaluation by an orthopedic) '
(physician or physical therapist for guided therapeutic exercises or knee) '
(aspiration if effusion persists.) '
() '
(Warning Signs and Urgent Orthopedic Red Flags: Seek prompt medical or emergency) '
(orthopedic attention if any of the following occur: complete inability to bear) '
(weight or take four consecutive steps; visible joint deformity or abnormal knee) '
(angle; a loud 'pop' followed by rapid, tense joint swelling within 2 hours) '
(\(suggesting hemarthrosis or cruciate rupture\); sensation of the knee catching,) '
(locking, or giving way entirely; severe redness, hot skin, high fever, and extreme) '
(joint tenderness \(raising suspicion of septic bacterial arthritis\); or sudden) '
(numbness, tingling, or cold pallor in the lower leg and foot.) '
() '
ET
endstream
endobj
