(GALE ENCYCLOPEDIA OF MEDICINE) '
(DIABETES MELLITUS AND GLYCEMIC REGULATION) '
() '
(Endocrine & Metabolic Health Disorders) '
() '
(Definition: Diabetes mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by) '
(persistent hyperglycemia resulting from defects in insulin secretion, insulin) '
(action, or both. It is broadly classified into Type 1 \(autoimmune destruction of) '
(pancreatic beta cells\) and Type 2 \(progressive insulin resistance accompanied by) '
(secretory defect\).) '
() '
(Causes and Pathophysiological Mechanisms: In Type 2 diabetes, peripheral tissues) '
(\(muscle, adipose, liver\) exhibit blunted sensitivity to circulating insulin,) '
(requiring compensatory hyperinsulinemia until beta cell exhaustion occurs.) '
(Contributing risk factors include genetic predisposition, abdominal adiposity,) '
(sedentary lifestyle, chronic systemic inflammation, and prolonged intake of) '
(refined carbohydrates.) '
() '
(Symptoms and Clinical Indicators: Classic symptoms include polyuria \(excessive) '
(urination\), polydipsia \(excessive thirst\), polyphagia \(constant hunger\),) '
(unintended weight loss, chronic fatigue, blurred vision, and delayed wound) '
(healing.) '
() '
(Evidence-Based Management and Prevention: Primary management combines medical) '
(nutrition therapy \(low glycemic index carbohydrates, dietary fiber, lean) '
(proteins\), at least 150 minutes of weekly moderate aerobic and resistance) '
(training, continuous glucose monitoring, and physician-prescribed oral) '
(hypoglycemics \(such as metformin\) or insulin regimens.) '
() '
ET
endstream
endobj
